WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.640
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.900
There are 25 horses

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.300
What is the minimum number of races needed so you can identify the fastest three horses?

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.800
You can race up to five horses at a time, but you do not have a watch

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:23.880
This problem is sometimes asked as a technical interview question at companies like Google

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:32.549
But since it is a little bit vague, let me present another version which has some details that allow you to solve the problem

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.440
There are 25 mechanical horses and a single race track

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:49.580
Each horse completes the track in a pre-programmed time and the horses all had different finishing times, unknown to you

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.440
You can race up to five horses at a time

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:07.720
After a race is over you get a printout with the order the horses finished, but not the finishing times of the horses

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:19.220
What is the minimum number of races you need to identify the fastest three of all the horses?

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.780
I was suggest to this problem by email from the puzzle maker Terry Stickels

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.360
And it is sometimes asked as an interview question at companies like Google

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.160
So can you figure it out?

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.780
Pause the video right now and give it a try

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:42.140
When you're ready keep watching the video for a solution

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:55.630
The answer is you can find the three fastest horses in a minimum of seven races

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:04.029
I will first describe the procedure in words, and then I will go over the solution in detail. So that you understand why it works

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:11.829
The first step is to divide the 25 horses into groups of five and race the horses in each group

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.529
This will be a set of five races

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.700
Next, take the winner from each group and race those five horses

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:26.380
The winner of this race, which is the winner of the winners is the fastest horse overall

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.770
Now in order to get the second and third fastest horses, we're going to have to do one more race

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:37.060
But to describe that race I'm going to have to present some notation

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:45.000
So label the five groups from step one as A, B, C, D, and E

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.420
to correspond to the groups for the horses that finished in

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.400
first, second, third, fourth, and fifth place for the race in step two

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.680
In other words, label the groups in Step one

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.520
according to the results of the winners race in step two

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.240
Furthermore, write a subscript to identify the order that the horse finished within a group

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.060
So A2 means the horse that finished second place in group A

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:20.660
So here's that final race. We do one more race with A2, A3, B1, B2, and C1

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.780
that is race the second and third fastest horses from group A

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:30.780
The fastest and second fastest horses from Group B. And the fastest horse from Group C

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.040
The top two finishers in this race will be the second and third fastest horses overall

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:44.660
So now I'm going to show you this procedure in detail and explain why it is the correct procedure

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.720
So we start up by dividing the 25 horses into groups of five

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:56.160
In this race we have a race of five horses and I'm going

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.780
to illustrate it so that the slowest horse is on the left and the fastest horse is on the right

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.080
I'll rearrange the horses if necessary

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.020
so that it follows that the slowest horse is on the left and the fastest horse is on the right

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.880
I'll do this for all the horses in all of the different groups

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:22.340
Now, we are going to take the winner from each group and race those five horses

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.280
This will be the horse on the far right hand side of each group

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:34.060
The winner of this race, which will be the winner of the winners, will then be the fastest horse overall

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.000
I am again going to draw this so that the

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.260
slowest of the winners is on the bottom and the fastest of the winners is on the top

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.390
We'll rearrange the groups as necessary to make this diagram work

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.840
So now we have the fastest of the winners

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.560
This will be the fastest horse overall

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.860
We know that it's beat every other horse within this group,

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.780
and it's beat the fastest horse in every other group

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.500
So we have identified the fastest horse overall

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.240
Now in order to get to the second and third fastest horses,

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.160
We'll have to identify the different groups

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.600
so we've the fastest group being A and the slowest group being E

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:23.220
So how are we going to get the second and third fastest overall?

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.240
We're going to do this by process of elimination

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.940
We can eliminate any horse that is slower than at least three other horses

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.860
If a horse is slower than at least three other horses

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.080
There's no way it could be the second or third fastest overall

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:44.220
So to start out with, we can eliminate all the horses in group E

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:51.520
Every single horse in this group will be slower than the fastest horses in groups A, B, and C

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:58.040
This is because the fastest horse was slower than the fastest horses in these groups A, B, and C

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:05.700
So all of the horses in groups, in group E, cannot possibly be the second or third fastest overall

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:11.499
For exactly the same reason we can eliminate all horses in group D

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.700
All of them are slower than A1, B1, and C1

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:27.040
We can continue this logic, and we can eliminate all the horses in Group C, except for the fastest horse

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:31.340
All of these other horses will be slower than A1, B1, and C1

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:40.940
Now in Group B, we can eliminate all the horses except for the two fastest horses B1 and B2

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:46.040
All of them will again be slower than A1, B1, and then B2

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.460
So the horses in third, fourth, and fifth place in Group B can be eliminated

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.120
From group A, we can eliminate the 5th and 4th fastest horses

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.400
because they will be slower than the top 3 horses within their own group

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:11.560
We're left with 6 horses. We can only race five at a time, but we can actually get rid of one of these horses

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.700
We've already identified the fastest horse overall, A1, so we don't need to race it anymore

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:22.560
We already know it's the fastest overall we can't learn any more information by racing it

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.720
So we can actually remove this horse from our race

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:34.120
So we now have A2, A3, B1, B2 ,and C1

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.780
and we do not know the relative speeds of these different horses

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:42.280
So we can do one more race with these five horses

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.420
The winner of this race will be the second fastest horse overall

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.700
and the runner-up will then be the third fastest overall

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:57.100
So these will be 7 races which will allow us to identify the three fastest horses

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:04.380
Now just a quick note. We've shown that 7 races is sufficient. I just want to explain why it's minimal

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.140
We obviously have to race each of the 25 horses once

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.800
And since we can race 5 horses at a time

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.180
that means we're going to at least start out with 25 over 5, which is 5 races

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:21.280
We're then going to need to compare the winners, which will be one more race,

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:25.960
And we're then going to have to compare at least the second place from group A

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.280
to the fastest horse in Group B

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:32.600
amongst other possible comparisons in order to find out the second fastest horse

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:39.180
So we know at least seven races will be needed and we've shown seven races is sufficient

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:42.160
Therefore the described procedure is optimal

